{"ep_id": "13848868:e1", "key_id": "13848868", "comp": "ranzcr-clip-catheter-line-classification", "tier": "Grandmaster", "episode": {"start_v": 1, "end_v": 6, "objective": "Establish baseline inference pipeline and tune fold count, image size, and rank-conversion postprocessing for initial ResNeXt model", "lever": "fold_count, image_resize, rank_conversion postprocessing", "pattern": "sweep", "outcome": "Best score 0.93873 at v5 with 5 folds, 448x448, rank off; rank conversion consistently hurts slightly", "n_transitions": 5, "n_scored": 5}, "items": [{"instance": {"goal": "Does converting predicted probabilities to ranks before averaging across folds improve the AUC-based leaderboard score?", "context": "The author toggled CONVERT_TO_RANK on/off across five submissions with a ResNeXt model. At 4 folds (384→448 image size), rank-averaging hurt by 0.00019. At 5 folds (448 image size), rank-averaging hurt by 0.00004. At the initial 5-fold 384 setting, rank-averaging helped by 0.00065.", "action": "Tested rank-conversion postprocessing repeatedly under different fold counts and image sizes. The effect was inconsistent and tiny; raw probability averaging was slightly better in most configurations. The author kept raw averaging as the default going forward."}, "general": {"goal": "Does rank-averaging of fold predictions improve a ranking metric compared to raw probability averaging?", "context": "When ensembling multiple cross-validation folds of the same model architecture, the choice between averaging raw probabilities and averaging ranks can affect the final metric, but the direction of the effect may depend on other settings (number of folds, input resolution).", "action": "Toggle rank-conversion on and off as a binary flag while holding other settings constant. If the effect is inconsistent across configurations and the magnitude is very small (order of 0.0001–0.001), prefer the simpler raw-averaging approach and move on to higher-impact levers."}, "steps": ["13848868:1->2", "13848868:3->4", "13848868:5->6"]}, {"instance": {"goal": "How many folds should be averaged and at what image resolution to maximize the ensemble's leaderboard AUC?", "context": "Starting from 5-fold averaging at 384×384 (LB ~0.930), the author tried 4-fold at 448×448 (LB 0.937), then 5-fold at 448×448 (LB 0.939). Each change was submitted separately to isolate the effect.", "action": "Increased image size from 384 to 448 and changed fold count from 5 to 4 simultaneously, gaining +0.007. Then added the 5th fold back, gaining another +0.002. The best result used all 5 folds at the larger 448 resolution, showing both more folds and larger images helped."}, "general": {"goal": "What combination of input resolution and number of ensemble folds maximizes the evaluation metric?", "context": "An initial baseline uses a moderate resolution and a standard fold count. Increasing resolution improves per-model quality; adding more folds reduces variance. Both changes are cheap to test at inference time when pretrained fold checkpoints already exist.", "action": "First increase the input resolution (which typically has the larger effect), then sweep the number of folds upward. Use all available folds unless a specific fold degrades the score. Confirm each change with a leaderboard submission since the two levers can interact."}, "steps": ["13848868:2->3", "13848868:4->5"]}], "error": null, "raw": null, "model": "wine-claude-opus-4-6"}
{"ep_id": "13848868:e2", "key_id": "13848868", "comp": "ranzcr-clip-catheter-line-classification", "tier": "Grandmaster", "episode": {"start_v": 6, "end_v": 10, "objective": "Add SSE attention block and hidden layer+dropout to model head, iterating rank conversion each time", "lever": "model head architecture (SSEBlock, hidden layer, dropout)", "pattern": "build", "outcome": "Best score 0.94330 at v10; SSE block and extra head layers improved score substantially", "n_transitions": 4, "n_scored": 4}, "items": [{"instance": {"goal": "Can enriching the classification head of a pretrained backbone (ResNeXt via timm) with attention and hidden layers improve multi-label AUC on chest X-ray classification?", "context": "Baseline 5-fold ensemble with a simple pooling+linear head scored 0.93873. The backbone was already strong (ResNeXt), so gains had to come from the head or post-processing.", "action": "Added an SSE (Squeeze-and-Excitation style) spatial-channel attention block before pooling (v7, +0.00266), then added a hidden Linear+ReLU+Dropout(0.5) layer between pooling and the final classifier (v9, +0.00135). Each architectural change was submitted first without rank conversion, then with rank conversion toggled on to measure its independent effect."}, "general": {"goal": "Where can further gain come from when a strong pretrained backbone has plateaued and changing the backbone is not an option?", "context": "The backbone features are powerful but the classification head is minimal (global pool → linear). The metric is ranking-based (AUC), so better-calibrated or more expressive heads can help.", "action": "Add a channel/spatial attention block before pooling and a hidden fully-connected layer with nonlinearity and dropout between pooling and the output. Introduce one component at a time and submit to isolate each component's contribution."}, "steps": ["13848868:6->7", "13848868:8->9"]}, {"instance": {"goal": "Does rank-averaging fold predictions help or hurt when the model head architecture changes?", "context": "Across the whole notebook history, toggling CONVERT_TO_RANK on/off gave inconsistent results: sometimes it helped (v1→v2, v7→v8, v9→v10), sometimes it hurt (v3→v4, v5→v6). Each model-head change was first submitted without rank conversion, then re-submitted with it.", "action": "After every architectural change to the head, the author submitted once with raw probability averaging and once with rank-based averaging. In this episode rank conversion consistently added a small gain (+0.00031 and +0.00029), so it was kept on. The pattern: always re-test rank conversion after any model change rather than assuming the previous finding still holds."}, "general": {"goal": "How should a post-processing choice (e.g., rank-based vs. raw-probability fold aggregation) be validated when the upstream model changes?", "context": "A binary post-processing toggle has shown inconsistent benefit across different model configurations. Its effect may depend on the score distribution produced by the current model.", "action": "Re-evaluate the toggle after every model or pipeline change by submitting both variants. Do not carry forward the previous conclusion; treat it as a cheap A/B test that must be re-run whenever upstream predictions change."}, "steps": ["13848868:7->8", "13848868:9->10"]}], "error": null, "raw": null, "model": "wine-claude-opus-4-6"}
{"ep_id": "13848868:e3", "key_id": "13848868", "comp": "ranzcr-clip-catheter-line-classification", "tier": "Grandmaster", "episode": {"start_v": 10, "end_v": 12, "objective": "Test whether increasing image size to 512x512 with simplified head improves score", "lever": "image_resize to 512x512", "pattern": "probe", "outcome": "Score dropped to 0.93722/0.93790; abandoned — 512x512 with simplified head hurt badly", "n_transitions": 2, "n_scored": 2}, "items": [{"instance": {"goal": "Will increasing image resolution from 448×448 to 512×512 improve the macro-averaged AUC for catheter/line classification?", "context": "At v10 the model used 448×448 images with an enriched head (hidden layer + dropout + SSE block) and rank-averaging, scoring 0.94330. The author simultaneously simplified the head (removed intermediate layers) and increased resolution to 512×512.", "action": "Changed IMAGE_SIZE to (512,512) and commented out the intermediate head layers. Score dropped sharply from 0.94330 to 0.93722, indicating the change was harmful. The author then tried rank conversion (v12, 0.93790) but could not recover the loss, and in hindsight reverted to a smaller image size in v13."}, "general": {"goal": "Will increasing input resolution improve classification accuracy when model weights were trained at a different resolution?", "context": "Pretrained weights and fold checkpoints were produced at a specific resolution. Changing the inference resolution without retraining (or without matching the training resolution) can create a distribution mismatch.", "action": "If testing a resolution increase at inference time, change only the resolution and keep all other architecture details (head layers, post-processing) identical so the effect can be isolated. If the score drops, revert; do not assume higher resolution is always better when weights were trained at a different size."}, "steps": ["13848868:10->11"]}, {"instance": {"goal": "When a configuration change causes a large score drop, how much can rank-based post-processing recover?", "context": "Going from v10 (0.94330) to v11 (0.93722) lost 0.006 by changing resolution and head simultaneously. Previous experiments consistently showed rank conversion adds roughly +0.001–0.003.", "action": "Enabled CONVERT_TO_RANK in v12, recovering only +0.00067 (to 0.93790). This confirmed rank conversion cannot compensate for a fundamentally worse model configuration, and the author reverted the resolution/head changes in subsequent versions."}, "general": {"goal": "Can a small post-processing trick compensate for a large regression caused by a model or preprocessing change?", "context": "A post-processing step (e.g., converting predictions to ranks before averaging) has historically provided only marginal gains on the order of a small fraction of the total score range.", "action": "Do not rely on post-processing to rescue a bad architectural or preprocessing decision. If a change causes a score drop much larger than the typical post-processing gain, revert the change rather than trying to patch it with post-processing."}, "steps": ["13848868:11->12"]}, {"instance": {"goal": "How can the effect of resolution vs. head architecture be diagnosed when both are changed at once?", "context": "In v10→v11 the author changed image size (448→512) and removed intermediate head layers simultaneously, making it impossible to attribute the 0.006 drop to either change alone.", "action": "The experiment was inconclusive about which factor caused the regression. In hindsight (v13) the author reverted both and re-added the head layers, suggesting the confounded change wasted two submission slots. Changing one variable at a time would have been more informative."}, "general": {"goal": "How to efficiently identify which modification is responsible for a score change when submission budget is limited?", "context": "Multiple changes (input preprocessing and model architecture) were applied in a single submission, and the resulting score drop could not be attributed to either one.", "action": "Change only one variable per submission. When submission budget is tight, prioritize isolating the variable most likely to have a large effect first, then layer on secondary changes only after confirming the first is beneficial or neutral."}, "steps": ["13848868:10->11", "13848868:11->12"]}], "error": null, "raw": null, "model": "wine-claude-opus-4-6"}
